.TH DRAGONGL-ANALYZE-LOG 1 "@DRAGONGL_RELEASE_DATE@" "Dragon GL @DRAGONGL_VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dragongl-analyze-log \- what happened on a Dragon GL server, from its log
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dragongl-analyze-log
[\fB\-\-strict\fR]
[\fIlog\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dragongl-analyze-log
(the script
.I analyze_log.py
of the Dragon GL tools, in
.IR @DRAGONGL_PKGDATADIR@/tools )
reads the log of \fBdragongl_server\fR(1), the lines
.I [HH:MM:SS] [CATEGORY] text
it writes on its standard output (the journal, under systemd), from
.I log
or from the standard input, and reports:
.TP
.B problems
WARNING and ERROR lines, failed saves, corrupted or unreadable saves,
protocol violations, TLS and network failures, sanitizer reports, and a
server started again without a clean shutdown; a few lines of each kind
are shown.
.TP
.B players
logins, new characters, wrong passwords and lockouts, refused
connections, disconnections by reason, deaths by cause, levels gained.
.TP
.B server
starts, clean shutdowns, autosaves and the last \fB[PERF]\fR line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Exit with status 1 when the log shows a problem (a cron job, a CI step).
Without it the report is all and the status is 0.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B journalctl \-u dragongl-server \-o cat \-\-since today | dragongl-analyze-log
The log of today.
.TP
.B dragongl-analyze-log \-\-strict server.log
A saved log; status 1 if it shows a problem.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dragongl_server (1),
.BR dragongl-analyze-balance (1)
